+# Keep sessions in signed cookies
+
+For whoever changes login, deploys or the database. Sessions live in signed cookies after an unbounded session table
+filled the database disk during Saturday pickup. This record preserves why the shared database was chosen first
+and why a session store is no longer used.
+
+## Context
+
+In 2023 larder moved from one server to two behind a load balancer, and sessions stayed in each server's memory.
+Members were logged out whenever a request reached the other server, and every deploy logged out everyone; the pantry
+volunteers stopped deploying during opening hours. Postgres replaced that memory: every server could read the same
+sessions, and it was already operated for the app.
+
+[Issue 63](../../notes/issue-63.md) records the later failure: expired sessions were never deleted, leaving 2.1 million
+rows containing every login since 2023. The database disk filled and larder was down from 09:40 to 12:50 during
+Saturday pickup. Volunteers used a paper list, and eleven members left without their pantry orders because the
+volunteers could not find them. Removing the expired rows restored service.
+
+The replacement must survive deploys and requests reaching different servers, avoid another disk-filling session
+table, and keep the services and jobs volunteers operate down. Losing server-side logout of an individual member
+before expiry is acceptable with the [session lifetime](../features/sessions.md#lifetime).
+
+## Options
+
+- **Sticky sessions at the load balancer**: no code change; lost because a deploy still logs out everyone on the
+  restarted server.
+- **Redis as a session store**: fast and shared across servers; rejected in 2023 and again in issue 63 because it
+  adds a service only Tom can run.
+- **Postgres with a nightly expiry cleanup job**: the original shared store survives deploys and adds no service;
+  cleanup would prevent the table growing without bound. Left because it adds a job to watch and still requires a
+  database read on every request.
+- **Signed cookies**: chosen; any web server checks the member and expiry with the shared key, removing the session
+  store and its per-request query without adding a service or cleanup job.
+
+## Decision
+
+Keep the member and expiry in a signed cookie that every web server can verify with the same key. This meets the
+deploy and volunteer-operation constraints while removing the session table that caused the outage. See
+[sessions](../features/sessions.md) for the implemented behavior and key configuration, and
+[architecture](../architecture.md#how-they-connect) for the connections.
+
+## Consequences
+
+The `sessions` table was dropped when this shipped; session checks no longer depend on a database query. Deploys
+preserve logins while servers retain the shared key. There is no server-side session record to revoke for one
+member, so a cookie remains usable until expiry. Key rotation invalidates everyone's cookies; it is restricted to
+between pickups to avoid logging members out during pickup. The session feature page owns the
+[operational behavior](../features/sessions.md#key-and-logout).
