 ## Lifetime
 
-Each request renews the session for 30 minutes, `EXPIRY_MINUTES` in `larder/sessions.py`. Until March 2024 the limit
+Each request renews the session for 30 minutes, `EXPIRY_MINUTES` in
+[`larder/sessions.py`](../../larder/sessions.py). `sign(member_id, last_request)` sets the expiry to 30 minutes after
+that request; `verify(cookie, now)` returns the member id if the signature matches and the expiry has not passed,
+or `None` for a signature mismatch or a passed expiry. Until March 2024 the limit
 was 10 minutes: members packing a pantry order on a slow mobile connection were logged out before they confirmed it
 and lost what they had picked, and support logged 120 tickets about it that month. The limit was raised to 30
 minutes, the shortest that ended those tickets.
 
 ## Storage
 
-Sessions live in Postgres ([keep sessions in the database](../adrs/0001-keep-sessions-in-the-database.md)).
+The cookie carries JSON fields `member` (the member id) and `expires` (an ISO-formatted timestamp). Its value is the
+URL-safe base64 encoding of that JSON, a dot, and a hexadecimal HMAC-SHA256 signature over the JSON bytes. The payload
+is signed, not encrypted. Session data lives in the cookie; the web server verifies it locally. See the
+[architecture](../architecture.md#how-they-connect) for where sessions fit in the app.
+
+## Key and logout
+
+Every web server must use the same `LARDER_SESSION_KEY`, supplied through deploy secrets. The module reads it at
+import time; when unset, the code falls back to `dev-only`. A deploy with the same key preserves logins across
+servers. Changing the key invalidates all existing cookies, so rotate it only between pickups.
+
+There is no server-side mechanism to invalidate one member's cookie before it expires. The
+[decision record](../adrs/0001-keep-sessions-in-signed-cookies.md) explains why this limit was accepted and why key
+rotation is scheduled between pickups.
 
